[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memoization in React: useMemo, useCallback, and React.memo"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"React is a powerful JavaScript library for building user interfaces. Performance is a critical aspect of any React application. Re-rendering components unnecessarily can lead to performance bottlenecks. Memoization is a powerful optimization technique that can significantly improve the performance of React applications by caching the results of expensive function calls and reusing them when the same inputs occur again. React provides several built-in hooks and higher-order components to implement memoization: `useMemo`, `useCallback`, and `React.memo`."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Memoization"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Memoization is an optimization technique where you store the results of expensive function calls and reuse them when the same inputs occur again. This avoids redundant computations and improves performance, especially when dealing with computationally intensive operations or frequent re-renders."},{"order":5,"type":"heading","text":"useMemo"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"`useMemo` is a React hook that memoizes the result of a computation. It accepts a function that performs the computation and a dependency array. The function is only re-executed when one of the dependencies in the array changes. Otherwise, `useMemo` returns the cached value."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Syntax"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"const memoizedValue = useMemo(() => computeExpensiveValue(a, b), [a, b]);"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"- `computeExpensiveValue`: The function that performs the expensive computation.
- `[a, b]`: The dependency array. The function will only re-run if `a` or `b` changes."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Example"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState, useMemo } from 'react';

function ExpensiveCalculation({ number }) {
  const result = useMemo(() => {
    console.log('Calculating expensive result...');
    // Simulate an expensive calculation
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < number * 1000000; i++) {
      sum += i;
    }
    return sum;
  }, [number]);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Number: {number}</p>
      <p>Expensive Result: {result}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(1);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <ExpensiveCalculation number={count} />
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `ExpensiveCalculation` component calculates a sum based on the `number` prop. The `useMemo` hook ensures that the calculation is only performed when the `number` prop changes. If the `number` prop remains the same, the cached result is returned, avoiding the expensive calculation."},{"order":13,"type":"heading","text":"useCallback"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"`useCallback` is a React hook that memoizes a function definition. It returns a memoized version of the callback function that only changes if one of the dependencies has changed. This is useful when passing callbacks to optimized child components that rely on reference equality to prevent unnecessary re-renders."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Syntax"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"const memoizedCallback = useCallback(
  () => {
    doSomething(a, b);
  },
  [a, b],
);"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"- `() => { doSomething(a, b); }`: The callback function to be memoized.
- `[a, b]`: The dependency array. The callback function will only be re-created if `a` or `b` changes."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Example"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState, useCallback } from 'react';

function Button({ onClick, children }) {
  console.log('Button rendered');
  return (
    <button onClick={onClick}>{children}</button>
  );
}

// React.memo is used to prevent re-renders if props haven't changed
const MemoizedButton = React.memo(Button);

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const increment = useCallback(() => {
    setCount(c => c + 1);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <MemoizedButton onClick={increment}>Increment</MemoizedButton>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `increment` function is memoized using `useCallback`. This ensures that the `increment` function's reference only changes when its dependencies change (in this case, it has no dependencies, so it never changes). The `MemoizedButton` component, wrapped with `React.memo`, only re-renders when its props change. Without `useCallback`, the `increment` function would be recreated on every render of `App`, causing `MemoizedButton` to re-render unnecessarily."},{"order":21,"type":"heading","text":"React.memo"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"`React.memo` is a higher-order component (HOC) that memoizes a functional component. It prevents re-renders if the component's props haven't changed. By default, it does a shallow comparison of the props. You can also provide a custom comparison function."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Syntax"},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"const MemoizedComponent = React.memo(MyComponent, [areEqual(prevProps, nextProps)]);"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"- `MyComponent`: The functional component to be memoized.
- `areEqual(prevProps, nextProps)` (optional): A custom comparison function that returns `true` if the next props are equal to the previous props (meaning no re-render is needed), and `false` otherwise. If not provided, a shallow comparison is used."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Example"},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';

function MyComponent({ name, age }) {
  console.log('MyComponent rendered');
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Name: {name}</p>
      <p>Age: {age}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

const areEqual = (prevProps, nextProps) => {
  // Custom comparison logic
  return prevProps.name === nextProps.name && prevProps.age === nextProps.age;
};

const MemoizedComponent = React.memo(MyComponent, areEqual);

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <MemoizedComponent name="John" age={25 + count} />
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `MyComponent` is memoized using `React.memo`. The `areEqual` function provides a custom comparison logic.  `MyComponent` will only re-render if either the `name` or `age` props change.  Without `React.memo`, `MyComponent` would re-render every time the `App` component re-renders, even if the props haven't changed."},{"order":29,"type":"heading","text":"When to Use Memoization"},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Expensive Computations: Use `useMemo` when you have computationally intensive operations that you want to avoid re-running unnecessarily."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Passing Callbacks: Use `useCallback` when passing callbacks to optimized child components that rely on reference equality."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Preventing Re-renders: Use `React.memo` to prevent functional components from re-rendering if their props haven't changed."},{"order":33,"type":"heading","text":"Important Considerations"},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Memoization comes with a cost: storing the cached values. Don't overuse it, as it can increase memory consumption.  Only memoize when the performance gains outweigh the memory overhead."},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always provide a dependency array to `useMemo` and `useCallback`. Omitting the dependency array can lead to unexpected behavior and performance issues."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be mindful of the comparison logic used by `React.memo`. The default shallow comparison might not be sufficient for complex objects. In such cases, provide a custom comparison function."},{"order":37,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"Memoization is a valuable technique for optimizing React applications. By using `useMemo`, `useCallback`, and `React.memo` effectively, you can prevent unnecessary re-renders and improve the overall performance of your application. However, it's crucial to use memoization judiciously and consider the trade-offs between performance gains and memory consumption."}]
